I found that annoying, too, until they introduced the snapshot.auto-track configuration option. Here&#x27;s an example for a basic Rust project:<p><pre><code>  [snapshot]
  auto-track = &quot;&quot;&quot;
    root-glob:&quot;{src,tests}&#x2F;**&#x2F;*.rs&quot; |
    root-glob:&quot;Cargo.*&quot; |
    root-file:&quot;README.md&quot;
  &quot;&quot;&quot;
</code></pre>
I set it to `snapshot.auto-track = &quot;none()&quot;` in the global config so that it is turned off by default and then selectively adjust it for a project.<p>You can also leave it as `none()` and then manually add files as before.<p>Note also that this has nothing to do with the staging area, but with selecting which files to add to a repo, which other VCSes can do just fine without having a staging area.<p>In fact, selective auto-track plus <i>not</i> having to explicitly stage changes makes it less of a risk that you forget to add something.

